   241	  are fixed.  Its transitive, transposition-generated image is the natural
   242	  \(S_3\) on that orbit (`REV:96`).  A desk peripheral lemma adds that, after
   243	  the trivial \(D_B\)-meridians are killed, the distinguished cusp-branch
   244	  longitude maps to one.  Indeed it commutes with a cusp meridian, has zero
   245	  cusp-meridian exponent and hence even image, while
   246	  \(C_{S_3}((12))\cap A_3=\{1\}\).  This is a local peripheral statement, not
   247	  a statement about the loop at infinity; any linking contributions from an
   248	  incident \(D_B\) die under \(\rho\).
   249	- In S1b, the invariant orbits are exactly \(O_3\sqcup O_2\), and the review
   250	  pins projections \(S_3\) and \(C_2\) (`REV:97`).  The following desk upgrade
   251	  explicitly supplies the inertia step requested there.  The reviewed
   252	  branch/cluster dictionary makes singular-branch Wirtinger meridians
   253	  \((\text{transposition},1)\) on the actual orbits; they generate
   254	  \(S_3\times1\).  A smooth-branch meridian is
   255	  \((1,\text{nontrivial})\).  Hence the full local image is
   256	  \(S_3\times C_2\).  No five-letter local orbit is possible.
