   181	satisfies `k ≤ 4`, and the remaining budget after the distinguished trivial is 3.
   182	
   183	At least one dicritical has `mu ≥ 2`. *Reason* `[D]`: the meridian of `D_i` acts
   184	on the 5 sheets with cycle type `1^{a_i} Π_{l→i} mu_l^{s_l}` (Lemma C, §3.3); if
   185	every `mu_l = 1` every meridian is trivial, and since `pi_1(C^2 − A_F)` is
   186	generated by meridians (Zariski–van Kampen) the covering
   187	`C^2 \ F^{-1}(A_F) → C^2 \ A_F` would be trivial, forcing `N = 1`. This is the
   188	Zariski–Nagata form of the promoted branch-locus nonemptiness (ALLN:190–211,
   189	C19 §1).
   190	
   191	Partitioning the residual 3 subject to `mu = 1 ⟹ corr = 0`:
   192	
   193	| Profile | cost multiset | `k` | dicritical data `(mu, corr)` |
   194	|---|---|---|---|
   195	| **P1** | `3 + 1` | 2 | `(2,1)` and `(1,0)` |
   196	| **P2** | `3 + 1` | 2 | `(3,0)` and `(1,0)` |
   197	| **P3** | `2 + 1 + 1` | 3 | `(2,0)`, `(1,0)`, `(1,0)` |
   198	
   199	`(1,2)` is excluded pointwise by (G4); `(2,0)+(1,1)` likewise; `(1,0)^{×4}` is
   200	excluded by branch-locus nonemptiness. This reproduces exactly the list (5.3)
   201	of ALLN §5.2 and REV §6.2, which was left with "target-component incidence and
   202	cover degrees still undetermined". Those are now determined.
   203	
   204	### 2.2 Cover degrees are forced
   205	
   206	Lemma B gives `s_l ≤ 1 + corr_l / mu_l` for every dicritical, hence:
   207	
   208	- P1: trivial `s = 1`; the `(2,1)` carrier has `2(s − 1) ≤ 1`, so **`s = 1`** and
   209	  `K_l = corr_l − mu_l(s_l − 1) = 1`.
   210	- P2: `(3,0)` gives `3(s − 1) ≤ 0`, so **`s = 1`**; `K_tot = 0`.
   211	- P3: `(2,0)` gives **`s = 1`**; all three have `s = 1`; `K_tot = 0`.
   212	
   213	So **every dicritical in every `N = 5` reducible profile carrying a trivial
   214	dicritical has `s_l = 1`** `[D]`. Consequence: by Lemma A each `h_l` is an
   215	isomorphism, `phi_l` is a birational parametrization of its component, and the
   216	promoted *ramified-nonprimitive escape* — the exact residue that leaves
   217	`OPEN[B0-H2-N>=20/…]` alive in the irreducible case — **cannot occur at `N = 5`
   218	in the reducible case at all.** The `N ≥ 5` reducible gap is therefore not a
   219	ramified-cover gap at `N = 5`; it is purely a component-incidence and
   220	`pi_1`-representation gap. (At `N ≥ 6` the floor stops forcing `s = 1`: the
   221	first reducible profile with a ramified carrier is `(2,2)+(1,0)` at `N = 7`,
   222	`s = 2`, `K = 0`.)
   223	
   224	Consistency with (AGG): P1 `Σ W_i = 2 + 1 = 3`, `K_tot = 1`, total 4 ✓;
   225	P2 `3 + 1 + 0 = 4` ✓; P3 `2 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 4` ✓.
   226	
   227	### 2.3 Incidence: which component owns the trivial dicritical
   228	
   229	Each component is the image of at least one dicritical (promoted "distinct owners
   230	per component"), so the assignment `tau` is onto `{1,…,m}` and `2 ≤ m ≤ k`.
   231	Enumerating all surjections up to relabelling of like dicriticals:
   232	
   233	| ID | profile | `m` | ownership | `(W_i, a_i)` per component |
   234	|---|---|---|---|---|
   235	| **P1** | `(2,1)+(1,0)` | 2 | `D_A ← (2,1,s=1)`, `D_B ← (1,0,1)` | `(2,3)`, `(1,4)` |
   236	| **P2** | `(3,0)+(1,0)` | 2 | `D_A ← (3,0,1)`, `D_B ← (1,0,1)` | `(3,2)`, `(1,4)` |
   237	| **P3a** | `(2,0)+(1,0)+(1,0)` | 3 | `D_A ← (2,0,1)`; `D_{B1}`, `D_{B2}` one trivial each | `(2,3)`, `(1,4)`, `(1,4)` |
   238	| **P3b** | same | 2 | `D_A ← (2,0,1)`; `D_B ← both trivials` | `(2,3)`, `(2,3)` |
   239	| **P3c** | same | 2 | `D_1 ← (2,0,1)+(1,0,1)`; `D_2 ← (1,0,1)` | `(3,2)`, `(1,4)` |
   240	
   241	That is the complete list: with `k = 2` a surjection onto `m = 2` forces the two
   242	dicriticals onto distinct components (P1, P2); with `k = 3` and `m = 3` we get
   243	P3a, and with `m = 2` the only two partition shapes are `{A}{B1,B2}` = P3b and
   244	`{A,B1}{B2}` = P3c (the relabelled `{A,B2}{B1}` is the same configuration).
   245	All entries satisfy `a_i ≥ 1`.
   246	
   247	**Structural corollary `[D]` (unique branched component).** In every row above
   248	exactly one component has a dicritical with `mu ≥ 2`, hence exactly one
   249	component has nontrivial meridian. *Reason*: a second branched component would
   250	need a second `mu ≥ 2` dicritical, costing `≥ 2`, and `1 + 2 + 2 = 5 > 4`.
   251	So at `N = 5`, in the reducible-with-trivial scope, **the branch locus of the
   252	covering is irreducible**. Every profile therefore reduces to a `pi_1` question
   253	about one irreducible polynomial curve — the same shape as `OPEN[PI1-S4]`, and
   254	not a genuinely multi-component representation problem. This is the single most
   255	useful structural fact produced by this lane.
   256	
   257	## 3. Per-component eta-criticality and the normalization question
   258	
   259	### 3.1 Which components inherit immersivity
   260	
   261	The promoted transfer ("every correction is ramification of `h_l`"; C19 §1.3)
   262	is derived from `d phi_l = d eta ∘ d h_l` **plus** immersivity of `eta`, and
   263	immersivity of `eta_i` is obtained from a trivial dicritical *over `D_i`*
   264	(ALLN §2). In the reducible case this is available only componentwise. Here is
   265	the exact inheritance at `N = 5`, using §2.2 (`s_l = 1` everywhere):
   266	
   267	- If `corr_l = 0` for some `l → D_i` with `s_l = 1`, then `h_l` is an
   268	  isomorphism and (G4) gives `d phi_l ≠ 0` everywhere, so `d eta_i ≠ 0`
   269	  everywhere: **`eta_i` is immersive, every branch of `D_i` is smooth, and every
   270	  singular point of `D_i` is multibranch.**
   271	- If `corr_l > 0` with `s_l = 1` (the single case P1, `corr = 1`), then
   272	  `h_l` is an isomorphism, so the criticality forced by (G4) has nowhere to go
   273	  except `d eta_i`: **`eta_i` is critical at exactly the correction parameters,
   274	  and `D_i` carries exactly that many singular branches.**
   275	
   276	So at `N = 5` the reducible case *inverts* the irreducible all-degree picture:
   277	because the floor kills every ramified carrier, `eta`-criticality is not
   278	scope-corrected away — it is exactly right, as it was at `N = 5` irreducible
   279	(REV §5). The promoted transfer correction is consumed here only as the
   280	statement that it is vacuous at this degree.
   281	
   282	### 3.2 The normalization question, answered
   283	
   284	Lemma A settles the charge's per-component normalization question outright: at
   285	`N = 5` (indeed at every degree) every component of `A_F` has `A^1`
   286	normalization and one place at infinity, i.e. is a **polynomial curve** in the
   287	PI1-S4 sense; with `s_l = 1` (§2.2) each is moreover *birationally* parametrized
   288	by its dicritical, so `eta_i = phi_i ∘ (h_i)^{-1}` is a proper polynomial
   289	parametrization `t ↦ (p_i(t), q_i(t))`. Consequently every promoted result whose
   290	hypothesis class is "irreducible polynomial curve, normalization `A^1`, one
   291	place at infinity, affine singularities of prescribed type" is *directly*
   292	applicable to the `N = 5` reducible residual — in particular Corollary N-A-RES
   293	and the (M-INF) cluster identity (CNA §1.2–1.3). That transferability is the
   294	main leverage this lane hands to the row machinery.
   295	
   296	### 3.3 Lemma C (meridian dictionary) `[D]`
   297	
   298	> **Lemma C.** Let `p` be a point of `D_i` and `gamma_i` a meridian of `D_i`.
   299	> Then `gamma_i` acts on the 5 sheets with cycle type
   300	> `1^{a_i} Π_{l→i} mu_l^{s_l}`. Locally at any `p ∈ A_F`, writing `G_p` for the
   301	> image of `pi_1(B_p \ A_F)`,
   302	> `#Fix(G_p) = a_p + (number of 1-cycles at infinity over p)`,
   303	> and the sheets not fixed are exactly those in the clusters counted by (LOC).
   304	
   305	*Proof.* Over a small transversal disc at a generic point of `D_i`, the `N`
   306	sheets split as: `a_i` affine preimages, at each of which `F` is a local
   307	isomorphism (Keller), hence fixed 1-cycles; and, for each `l → i` and each of
   308	the `s_l` points of `phi_l^{-1}(p)`, one cluster of `mu_l` sheets permuted
   309	cyclically by the local model `f^*(z,w) = (z + O(w), w^{mu_l}·unit)` of Orevkov
   310	Lemma 3.1. For the local statement at special `p`: an affine preimage `x` gives
   311	a component of `F^{-1}(B_p \ A_F)` mapped isomorphically (again since `F` is
   312	étale at `x`), i.e. a fixed letter; the remaining fixed letters are 1-cycles
   313	belonging to clusters at infinity, which by the same local model occur exactly
   314	when some `mu_l = 1`. ∎
   315	
   316	Two immediate `N = 5` gates:
   317	
   318	- **(M-1)** A component all of whose dicriticals have `mu = 1` has trivial
   319	  meridian; killing those meridians, the monodromy factors through
   320	  `pi_1(C^2 − D_br)` where `D_br` is the unique branched component (§2.3).
   321	- **(M-2)** `Im(pi_1(C^2 − A_F) → S_5)` is transitive, because
   322	  `C^2 \ F^{-1}(A_F)` is the complement of a curve in `C^2`, hence connected,
   323	  and it is the total space of the covering.
   324	
   325	## 4. Gates applied: profile-by-profile kill sheet
   326	
   327	### 4.1 Gate AMS: a smooth branched component is a coordinate line
   328	
   329	> **Gate AMS `[D]`.** Suppose the branched component `D_br` has
   330	> `corr_l = 0` for all `l → D_br` and `W_br ≥ 3`. Then `D_br` is smooth,
   331	> `eta_br` is a closed embedding, `D_br` is a coordinate line of `C^2`
   332	> (Abhyankar–Moh–Suzuki), `pi_1(C^2 − D_br) = Z`, the monodromy image is cyclic,
   333	> and by (M-1)+(M-2) it must be transitive on 5 letters — impossible, since a
   334	> cyclic group generated by a permutation of type `1^{a} Π mu^s` with `a ≥ 1` has
   335	> a fixed letter. **The profile dies.**
   336	
   337	*Detail.* `corr = 0` and `s = 1` give `eta_br` immersive (§3.1), so every branch
   338	is smooth; by (LOC-1) with `K_p = 0`, `r_{br,p} ≤ 5/W_br < 2`, so `D_br` is
   339	unibranch at every point, i.e. `eta_br` is injective and immersive, hence a
   340	closed embedding of `A^1`. AMS (Abhyankar–Moh 1975; Suzuki 1974 — classical,
   341	consumed without a local hashed PDF and flagged as such in §8) makes it a
   342	coordinate line, whose complement is `C^* × C`.
   343	
   344	**KILL P2.** `W_A = 3`, `corr_A = 0`: Gate AMS applies verbatim. (Its meridian
   345	type would have been `1^2 3`, a 3-cycle; the surviving cyclic image `Z/3` fixes
   346	two letters.) A cross-check without AMS: `deg D_A ≥ 2` from Gate TG below, while
   347	Gate AMS forces degree 1.
   348	
   349	**KILL P3c.** `D_1` carries `(2,0,1)` and `(1,0,1)`, so `W_1 = 3`, all
   350	`corr = 0`: Gate AMS applies verbatim. Its meridian type is
   351	`1^{a_1} · 2^1 · 1^1 = 1^3 2`, a transposition; the cyclic image `Z/2` is
   352	intransitive on 5 letters. The presence of the second component `D_2` does not
   353	help: killing its (trivial) meridians only moves the surjection to
   354	`pi_1(C^2 − D_1)`.
   355	
   356	Gate AMS is exactly the `N = 5` reason why **the trivial dicritical cannot share
   357	its component with the `mu ≥ 2` dicritical**: sharing pushes `W` to 3, which
   358	starves the fibre so hard that the component must be smooth.
   359	
   360	### 4.2 Gate LOC on the surviving branched component
   361	
   362	For the survivors `W_br = 2`, `a_br = 3`, and (LOC) at `p ∈ D_br` reads
   363	`a_p = 5 − 2 r_{br,p} − Σ_{j≠br} W_j r_{j,p} − K_p`. With `a_p ≥ 0`:
   364	
   365	- `r_{br,p} ≤ 2`: **`D_br` has no point of three or more branches.** In
   366	  particular the promoted `(6,4)` counterexample curve of CNA §1.3 (`beta_h = 15`
   367	  via an *ordinary triple point*) is out of class here for the same reason it was
   368	  out of class at `N = 4`.
   369	- at a 2-branch point with `K_p = 0`: `a_p = 1`;
   370	- at a 2-branch point with `K_p = 1`: `a_p = 0`;
   371	- at a point of `D_br ∩ D_j` (`W_j = 1`): `a_p = 5 − 2r_{br,p} − r_{j,p} − K_p`,
   372	  so (LOC-2) permits the intersection and no incidence is excluded.
   373	
   374	**Finding `[D]`: component incidence is not an obstruction at `N = 5`.** Every
   375	incidence pattern allowed by (LOC-2) is arithmetically consistent; the charge's
   376	"component-incidence" half of the OPEN name is answered negatively — the gap is
   377	entirely in the `pi_1` half.
   378	
   379	### 4.3 Gate TG: the transversal-genus identity
   380	
   381	> **Gate TG `[D]`.** Let `L ⊂ C^2` be a generic line in the target,
   382	> `S_i := Σ_{l→i} s_l`, and let `Σ_∞ := Σ_j sigma_j` where the sum is over the
   383	> divisor components at infinity mapping *onto* `L_∞`, with degrees `sigma_j`
   384	> and multiplicities `nu_j` (so `Σ_j sigma_j nu_j = N`, whence `1 ≤ Σ_∞ ≤ N`).
   385	> Then, with `g` the genus of the smooth model of `F^{-1}(L)`,
   386	>
   387	> ```text
   388	> 2g + Σ_∞  =  2 − N + Σ_i (deg D_i) · Σ_{l→i} s_l (mu_l − 1).
   389	> ```
   390	
   391	*Proof.* `F^{-1}(L)` is smooth (`F` is étale) and connected: by Zariski's
   392	hyperplane-section theorem `pi_1(L \ A_F) ↠ pi_1(C^2 − A_F)` for generic `L`, so
   393	the monodromy on `L` is still transitive by (M-2), making `F^{-1}(L \ A_F)`
   394	connected and dense in `F^{-1}(L)`. Multiplicativity of `chi_c` over the
   395	`N`-sheeted covering plus the fibre counts gives
   396	`chi(F^{-1}(L)) = N(1 − Σ_i deg D_i) + Σ_i a_i deg D_i = N − Σ_i W_i deg D_i`
   397	(a generic `L` meets `D_i` in `deg D_i` points, all generic on `D_i`). The
   398	places at infinity of `F^{-1}(L)` are: over each of the `deg D_i` points of
   399	`L ∩ D_i`, one branch at each of the `S_i` points of the dicritical fibre — one,
   400	not more, because the local model `f^*(z,w) = (z + O(w), w^{mu} · unit)` pulls a
   401	transversal back to `z = const + λ^{-1} w^{mu}(…)`, a single smooth branch; plus,
   402	over the generic point `q_∞ = L̄ ∩ L_∞`, one branch at each of the `Σ_∞` points
   403	of the fibre of the `L_∞`-dicriticals. Generic `L` avoids the finitely many
   404	image points of contracted components. So `b = Σ_i S_i deg D_i + Σ_∞`, and
   405	`chi = 2 − 2g − b` closes the identity. ∎
   406	
   407	*Control.* For `N = 1` (an automorphism, `A_F = ∅`, one `L_∞`-dicritical with
   408	`sigma = nu = 1`) the identity reads `0 + 1 = 2 − 1 + 0` ✓, and `chi = 1` ✓.
   409	
   410	At `N = 5` with all `s_l = 1` and a single `mu ≥ 2` dicritical over `D_br`:
   411	
   412	```text
   413	(deg D_br) · (mu_br − 1) = 3 + 2g + Σ_∞ ≥ 4.
   414	```
   415	
   416	So for the survivors (`mu_br = 2`): **`deg D_br = 3 + 2g + Σ_∞ ≥ 4`**, in *any*
   417	coordinate system, hence also `d_min ≥ 4` in the sense of the promoted
   418	D1-DEGREE typing (CNA §1.4; raw-degree phrasings are barred, so the payoff is
   419	stated for `d_min` as well as for the ambient degree).
   420	
   421	**Independent confirmation of `deg ≥ 4` `[D]`.** By Zariski's theorem
   422	`pi_1(C^2 − D_br)` is generated by the `d = deg D_br` meridians of a generic
   423	line; their images are transpositions generating a transitive subgroup of `S_5`,
   424	so the graph on 5 vertices whose edges are those transpositions is connected and
   425	needs `≥ 4` edges. Hence `d ≥ 4`. Two structurally independent routes (Euler
   426	characteristic vs. generation) give the same bound; at `N = 4` the same two
   427	arguments give `d ≥ 3`, consistent with the promoted `S_4` survivor list which
   428	begins at `(4,3)`.
   429	
   430	### 4.4 Gate N-A-RES (transfer of the promoted row machinery)
   431	
   432	Corollary N-A-RES (CNA §1.2) is a statement about a curve, not about `N`: for
   433	`D` an irreducible polynomial curve all of whose singularities are double points
   434	of two smooth branches, `M_∞ + 2T ≤ 3d − 3` implies `pi_1(C^2 − D) = Z`. By
   435	§3.2 the surviving branched component of P3a/P3b is exactly in that class, so:
   436	
   437	> **Gate N-A-RES at `N = 5` `[P]`.** Whenever the promoted gate fires,
   438	> `pi_1(C^2 − D_br) = Z`, the image is cyclic, and transitivity on 5 letters
   439	> fails. The profile dies.
   440	
   441	Standing correction consumed: use `4T` rather than `2T` as the tangential charge
   442	until `OPEN[NORI-BC-SELF-TANGENT-COEFF]` settles (CNA §3); at `T = 0` the gate
   443	is verbatim (M-INF), which is the promoted-as-corrected cluster identity
   444	`M_emb = mult + beta_h − 1`. What does **not** transfer is every ROW-SWEEP kill
   445	that consumed the `S_4` Main Theorem — specifically the two degree-six
   446	tangential kills `(6,3)-(7,4)` and `(6,3)-(8,3)`, which reduce to a *coprime*
   447	row and then invoke "no surjection onto `S_4` with transposition meridians".
   448	There is no promoted `S_5` analogue, so **at `N = 5` the coprime stratum is
   449	wide open** even where it was closed at `N = 4`. The `N = 5` residual is
   450	strictly larger than the `N = 4` residual, not smaller.
   451	
   452	### 4.5 Kill sheet
   453	
   454	| profile | gate | verdict |
   455	|---|---|---|
   456	| `(1,0)^{×4}` (no `mu ≥ 2`) | Zariski–Nagata / (M-2) | KILLED |
   457	| `(2,0)+(1,1)`, `(1,2)` | (G4) pointwise | KILLED |
   458	| any `s_l ≥ 2` | Lemma B floor | EXCLUDED (no such profile exists at `N = 5`) |
   459	| two branched components | budget (§2.3) | EXCLUDED |
   460	| **P2** `(3,0)+(1,0)` | Gate AMS (`W = 3`, `corr = 0`) | **KILLED** |
   461	| **P3c** `(2,0)+(1,0)` on one component | Gate AMS (`W = 3`) | **KILLED** |
